== Chapter 12

#align(center)[#smallcaps(all: true)[GOD DISTINGUISHED FROM IDOLS, THAT HE MAY BE THE EXCLUSIVE OBJECT OF WORSHIP.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. Scripture, in teaching that there is only one God, is not arguing about words, but gives all honor and religious worship to him alone. This is proved, first, by the derivation of the word; second, by the testimony of God himself, when he declares that he is a jealous God and will not allow himself to be confused with any fictitious deity.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. In opposing this pure doctrine, the papists gain nothing by their distinction between #emph[dulia] and #emph[latria].]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. Passages of Scripture that overturn the papists’ distinction and prove that religious worship is due to God alone. Perversions of divine worship.]

#v(0.8em)

1\. #smallcaps[We] said at the beginning of our work#footnote[Ch. 2. (Beveridge’s note.)] that the knowledge of God does not consist in cold speculation, but brings his worship with it; and we touched in passing#footnote[Ch. 5 s. 6, 9, 10. (Beveridge’s note.)] on what will be treated more fully in other places#footnote[Book 2, ch. 8. (Beveridge’s note.)]: namely, how God is rightly worshiped. Now I only briefly repeat that whenever Scripture asserts the unity of God, it is not contending for a bare name, but also commands that nothing which belongs to Divinity be applied to any other. This makes it plain how pure religion differs from superstition. The Greek word εὐσέβεια means “right worship”; for the Greeks, though groping in darkness, always knew that a fixed rule had to be kept, so that God would not be worshiped absurdly. Cicero rightly and shrewdly derives the name #emph[religion] from #emph[relego], and yet the reason he gives is forced and farfetched: namely, that honest worshipers #emph[read] and #emph[read again], and ponder what is true.#footnote[Cic. De Nat. Deor. lib. 2 c. 28. See also Lactant. Inst. Div. lib. 4 c. 28. (partly Beveridge’s note.)] I think rather that the name is set against #emph[wandering license]. For most of mankind rashly snatch up whatever first comes their way, and even flit from one thing to another, whereas piety, so that it may stand with a firm step, collects itself within its proper limits. In the same way, superstition seems to take its name from its not being content with the measure and rule prescribed, but piling up a needless heap of empty trifles. But to say no more about words: it has been agreed in every age that religion is corrupted and perverted whenever false opinions are brought into it. From this it follows that whatever is allowed to be done out of thoughtless zeal cannot be defended by any pretext the superstitious may choose to cloak it with. But although this confession is in every man’s mouth, a shameful stupidity shows itself at once, for men neither cling to the one God nor use any discernment in their worship, as we have already observed.

But God, to assert his own right, first proclaims that he is a jealous God and will be a stern avenger if he is confused with any false god; and then he defines what due worship is, so as to keep the human race in obedience. He includes both of these in his Law, when he first binds the faithful in allegiance to himself as their only Lawgiver, and then prescribes a rule for worshiping him according to his will. I will consider the Law, with its many uses and purposes, in its own place; for now I only touch on this one: that it is meant as a bridle to curb men and keep them from turning aside to spurious worship. But we must hold to what I said at the outset: unless everything proper to divinity is confined to God alone, he is robbed of his honor, and his worship is violated.

Here we must look more closely at the cunning tricks that superstition uses. In defecting to foreign gods, it avoids seeming to abandon the Supreme God, or to reduce him to the same rank as the others. It gives him the highest place, but at the same time surrounds him with a crowd of minor deities, among whom it divides up his own functions. In this way, though in a hidden and crafty manner, the glory of the Godhead is dissected and not allowed to remain whole. So too the people of old, both Jews and Gentiles, set an immense crowd under the father and ruler of the gods, and gave them, each according to his rank, a share with the supreme God in the government of heaven and earth. So too, for some centuries now, departed saints have been raised to partnership with God, to be worshiped, invoked, and praised in his place. And yet we do not even think that the majesty of God is obscured by this abomination, though it is in great part suppressed and extinguished; all we retain is a cold opinion of his supreme power. Meanwhile, deceived by these entanglements, we go astray after various gods.

2\. The distinction between what they call #emph[dulia] and #emph[latria] was invented for this very purpose: to allow divine honors to be paid to angels and dead men with apparent impunity. For it is plain that the worship the papists pay to the saints differs in no way from the worship of God, for they pay this worship to both without distinction. Only when they are pressed do they fall back on the evasion that what belongs to God is kept intact, because they leave him #emph[latria]. But since the question is about the thing, not the word, how can they be allowed to toy as they please with a matter of the greatest importance? But, to pass over this, the most they will gain by their distinction is that they give worship to God and service to the others. For λατρεία in Greek means the same as #emph[worship] in Latin, whereas δουλεία properly means service, though in Scripture the two words are sometimes used interchangeably. But even granting that the distinction is always kept, we must still ask what each means. Δουλεία unquestionably means service, and λατρεία worship. But no man doubts that to #emph[serve] is something higher than to #emph[worship]. For it would often be a hard thing to serve one whom you would not refuse to revere. It is, therefore, an unjust division to assign the greater to the saints and leave the lesser to God. But several of the ancient Fathers used this distinction. What of it, when all men see that it is not only improper but utterly frivolous?

3\. Setting subtleties aside, let us examine the thing itself. When Paul reminds the Galatians what they were before they came to the knowledge of God, he says that they “served those who by nature are not gods” (Gal 4:8). Because he does not say #emph[latria], was their superstition excusable? This superstition, to which he gives the name of #emph[dulia], he condemns as much as if he had given it the name of #emph[latria]. When Christ repels Satan’s insulting proposal with the words, “It is written, You shall worship the Lord your God” (Matt 4:10), there was no question of #emph[latria] by name. For all that Satan asked was προσκύνησις (homage). Likewise, when John is rebuked by the angel for falling on his knees before him (Rev 19:10)#footnote[See also Rev 22:8, 9. (Beveridge’s note.)], we should not suppose that John had so far forgotten himself as to mean to transfer to an angel the honor due to God alone. But because any worship joined with religion cannot help having something of divine worship about it, he could not προσκυνεῖν (do homage to) the angel without taking something from the glory of God. True, we often read that men were worshiped; but that was, so to speak, civil honor. It is different with religious honor, which, the moment it is joined with worship, brings a profaning of the divine honor along with it. The same thing may be seen in the case of Cornelius (Acts 10:25). He had not made so little progress in piety as not to give supreme worship to God alone. So when he prostrates himself before Peter, he certainly does not do it with the intention of worshiping him in place of God. Yet Peter sternly forbids him. And why? Only because men never distinguish so carefully between the worship of God and that of created things as not to transfer indiscriminately to created things what belongs to God alone. Therefore, if we want to have one God, let us remember that not even the smallest part of his glory may be taken from him, but that he must keep what is his own. So when Zechariah speaks of the restoring of the Church (Zech 14:9), he says expressly not only that there would be one God, but also that he would have only one name, so that he might have nothing in common with idols. The nature of the worship that God requires will be seen in its own place.#footnote[Book 2, c. 7 and 8. (Beveridge’s note.)] He has been pleased to prescribe in his Law what is lawful and right, and so to bind men to a fixed rule, so that no one would allow himself to devise a worship of his own. But since it is not wise to burden the reader by mixing many topics together, I do not dwell on this one now. Let it be enough to remember that whatever acts of piety are given to anyone other than God alone amount to sacrilege. First superstition gave divine honors to the sun and stars, or to idols; then came ambition, which, decking man out in the spoils of God, dared to profane all that was sacred. And though the principle of worshiping a supreme Deity was still held, the custom was to offer sacrifices indiscriminately to guardian spirits and minor gods, or to dead heroes. So easy is the slide into this vice of sharing with a crowd what God strictly claims as his own right alone!
